Part 4: Clone an existing GitHub repository

Cloning is different from initializing a repository.

Use git clone when you want to download an existing GitHub repository to your computer.

For example, you want a local copy of:

https://github.com/gauravb-2673/employee_management_lite_2.git
Step 1: Navigate to the destination directory

Suppose you want the project on your Desktop.

cd ~/Desktop

Check the current directory:

pwd
Step 2: Clone the repository

Run:

git clone https://github.com/gauravb-2673/employee_management_lite_2.git

Git will create a directory named:

employee_management_lite_2

The directory name is normally derived from the repository URL.

Clone into a custom directory

You can specify a different destination name:

git clone https://github.com/gauravb-2673/employee_management_lite_2.git employee-project

This creates a local directory named employee-project.

Step 3: Enter the cloned project

If you used the default directory name:

cd employee_management_lite_2

Check the files:

ls -la

Check the Git status:

git status

Inspect the remote:

git remote -v

Inspect the branches:

git branch -a

What did git clone do automatically?

It downloaded the repository and its history, initialized the local repository, configured the origin remote, and checked out the default branch.

You do not need to run git init or git remote add origin after a normal successful clone.

If the repository is private

You must authenticate with an account that has permission to access it.

Depending on your Git configuration, Git may use browser-based authentication, Git Credential Manager, or SSH.

Do not put a password or personal access token directly into a repository URL or commit it to your project.

Part 5: Pull the latest changes from GitHub

Suppose you cloned your Laravel project yesterday, and today another developer updated routes/web.php.

You want those changes on your computer.

Step 1: Enter the repository
cd ~/Desktop/employee_management_lite_2
Step 2: Check your current changes
git status

This is important because local modifications may conflict with incoming changes.

Commit or otherwise safely preserve important local work before integrating remote changes.

Step 3: Pull changes
git pull

A normal git pull fetches remote changes and integrates them into the current branch, using the configured upstream branch.

For a specific remote and branch:

git pull origin main

This explicitly pulls from origin/main.

Difference between git fetch and git pull

Fetch:

git fetch origin

Downloads remote information and updates remote-tracking references, without integrating those commits into your current branch.

Pull:

git pull origin main

Fetches the remote changes and integrates them into your current branch.

A useful rule is:

Use git fetch when you want to inspect remote changes first.
Use git pull when you are ready to integrate them.
