Part 7: Common branch commands

Branches let you develop a feature separately from your main code.

For example, you want to build a new employee search feature.

Step 1: View local branches
git branch

The current branch is marked with *.

Step 2: Create a new branch
git branch feature/employee-search

This creates the branch but does not switch to it.

Step 3: Switch branches
git switch feature/employee-search
Step 4: Create and switch in one command

This is the common shortcut:

git switch -c feature/employee-search

It creates the branch and switches to it.

Step 5: Commit and push the feature branch

After making changes:

git add .
git commit -m "Add employee search"
git push -u origin feature/employee-search

The first push establishes upstream tracking for that feature branch.

Step 6: Switch back to main
git switch main

To list local and remote branches:

git branch -a
Part 8: Fix common Git errors
Error 1: remote origin already exists

Cause: A remote named origin is already configured.

Inspect it:

git remote -v

If necessary, change its URL:

git remote set-url origin YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL

Replace YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL with the actual GitHub URL.

Error 2: src refspec main does not match any

This commonly happens when the branch does not exist locally or the repository has no commit yet.

Check your branch:

git branch

Check the status:

git status

If you have staged project files but have not made your first commit:

git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"

Then, if you intend to use main:

git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

Do not assume this is the correct fix without checking your current branch and repository state.

Error 3: ! [rejected] main -> main (non-fast-forward)

This was the error you encountered earlier.

Example:

! [rejected] main -> main (non-fast-forward)
error: failed to push some refs

Meaning: Your remote branch contains commits that your local branch does not contain. Git refuses to push because doing so would not be a fast-forward update.

Start by inspecting the repository:

git status
git fetch origin
git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all -10

If the branches share a history and the remote changes are safe to integrate:

git merge origin/main

Resolve any conflicts, commit the merge if Git requires it, and then push:

git push

If the histories are unrelated, as happened in your project, first inspect the remote commit:

git show --stat origin/main

If you want to retain both histories, you can merge them:

git merge origin/main --allow-unrelated-histories

Resolve any conflicts and then push:

git push -u origin main

Do not use git push --force as the default fix. It can overwrite remote history.

Error 4: Merge conflict

A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically reconcile competing changes.

Check the conflicted files:

git status

Open each conflicted file. You may see markers like:

<<<<<<< HEAD
Your local version
=======
The incoming version
>>>>>>> origin/main

Edit the file to retain the correct code and remove the conflict markers.

Then stage the resolved file:

git add path/to/file

For example:

git add README.md

Complete the merge:

git commit

Then push:

git push

Follow the exact instructions Git prints if it has already created a merge commit or if the operation is still in progress.

Error 5: Wrong Git username or email

Check the identity configured for your current project:

git config user.name
git config user.email

Check the global identity:

git config --global user.name
git config --global user.email

Correct the identity for the current repository:

git config user.name "Gaurav Bansode"
git config user.email "gauravb@parasightsolutions.com"

To correct the most recent commit's author before pushing it:

git commit --amend --reset-author --no-edit

Caution: Amending a commit changes its ID. If that commit has already been pushed and other people depend on it, coordinate before rewriting history.

Also, changing Git's author identity does not change which GitHub account is authenticated in VS Code or your terminal.

