Part 10: The two most important workflows
A. Existing Laravel project → GitHub

Use this when the project already exists locally.

cd ~/Desktop/employee_management_lite_2

git status

git init

git config user.name "Gaurav Bansode"
git config user.email "gauravb@parasightsolutions.com"

git add .

git commit -m "Initial commit: Employee Management Lite"

git branch -M main

git remote add origin https://github.com/gauravb-2673/employee_management_lite_2.git

git push -u origin main

Important: These commands assume the project does not already have the relevant Git history or remote configured, and that the GitHub repository is empty. If you have already initialized Git or configured origin, inspect the current state rather than repeating those commands blindly.

B. GitHub → Local computer

Use this when the repository already exists on GitHub and you want a local copy.

cd ~/Desktop

git clone https://github.com/gauravb-2673/employee_management_lite_2.git

cd employee_management_lite_2

git status

git remote -v

For a Laravel project, cloning downloads the source code and Git history, but you will normally need to install dependencies and configure the local environment before running the application.

For example:

composer install

Then create a local .env based on .env.example and configure the database and other environment-specific settings.

For a local Laravel application, you may also need to generate an application key:

php artisan key:generate

Run migrations only after confirming that the database configuration points to the correct local database:

php artisan migrate

Do not run php artisan migrate:fresh on a database containing data you want to preserve. It drops existing tables before recreating them.
